# Commit message format

When the user asks for a commit (or amends one), use **this repo’s subject-line shape** — not Conventional Commits essays or PR-style summaries.

## Shape

```text
<type>(<scope>): <message>
```

or without scope:

```text
<type>: <message>
```

- **`<type>`** — short prefix: `fix`, `feat`, `chore`, `ci`, … (lowercase).
- **`(<scope>)`** — optional; use for area: `ci`, `ui`, `entity-id`, `wave13`, package slug, etc.
- **`:` then exactly one space** — then the rest of the subject.
- **`<message>`** — one line, plain and specific (what changed / why in a few words). Match recent history: lowercase start is fine; no marketing tone, no multi-clause titles.

## Examples (from this repo)

```text
fix(ci): fix another build error
fix(entity-id): drizzle integration bug
chore(ci): fix double changeset
feat(wave13): ship @eristack/tax (F3)
chore(ui): site catalog and deep docs for erp ui stack
```

## Do not

- Subject lines like `Prepare ERP UI stack release: site, deep docs, and peer floors.` (no type prefix; reads like a PR blurb).
- Multi-paragraph commit bodies unless the user asks for a body.
- `git` at all unless the user explicitly requests a commit (see `no-git.mdc`).

## Picking type + scope

| Change | Typical prefix |
| --- | --- |
| CI / prepush / workflows / check scripts | `fix(ci):` or `chore(ci):` |
| Bug in a package | `fix(<package-slug>):` |
| New capability | `feat(<scope>):` |
| Docs / site / knowledge only | `chore(docs):` or `chore(ui):` |
| Release prep, peers, changesets metadata | `chore(release):` or `chore(ui):` |

When unsure, read `git log -15 --oneline` and match that voice.
